                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "This is a yes, but only to people who like classic Sierra/Monkey Island style item puzzles AND likes Metroidvanias.  If you're not fond of obscure item puzzles and hitting certain dialogue triggers you're not going to have a good time.\n\nVisually it looks nice, the sprites are large and animated nicely.  There's a good amount of enemy variety and terrain is very detailed, especially outside the school.  Sound's fine and music is good if repetitive.\n\nPlatforming is solid but you are very floaty and move a bit slow until you get a few abilities toward mid-game.  Combat is decent, and I do like that even though there's contact damage from enemies to you, you do contact damage back.  This damage can sometimes add up and be a very useful thing when you can collect enough healing hearts.  That said, some bosses are difficult to tell what hits you and what doesn't, and that leads to a lot of unnecessary damage.  Bosses are also very tanky and your thumb will get sore spamming attack on them.\n\nThere's levels, and your attack strengthens (and at certain thresholds changes) and health increases on each level.  If you die, you lose all XP progress toward the next level, so be sure to get your level before tackling a boss.  You cap out at level 8 so it's not too punishing if you die a few times.\n\nSome progression paths are difficult to figure out, they require a lot of items found and certain story dialogues triggered before you can proceed.\n\nThere's two endings, a standard ending and one with a true final boss (with an alternate combat form) if you collect all the lore items.  Both endings can be obtained on the same save if you trigger the ending without the lore items, then go get the last lore and do the rest.  The true final boss is a frustrating mess and soured me quite a bit on it.  It feels really janky to do, and it's very difficult to see what your hitbox is.  \n\nOverall the game is short, it's in the mini-Metroidvania category like Witch & the 66 Mushrooms or Tiny Dangerous Dungeons.  It took me 5 hours to 100% the game on a first playthrough (just missing the speedrun achievement which would need another run).  This is with getting stuck getting the right triggers and items together for a late game bit of progression.\n\nFinally, if you're a Linux or SteamDeck player, the native version wouldn't run, and I couldn't get it to run properly no matter what Steam runtime I tried.  I had to switch to the Windows version and use Proton to play it, which worked fine.\n\nOverall, yes with caveats, and you must like both traditional adventure games and combat heavy Metroidvanias to pick it up.",
